WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.000
Her hair is long and black.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.529
Her nails sharp.

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:05.149
She thirsts for blood.

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:09.750
Once, vibrant, alive, and pregnant—she’s
none of those things anymore.&nbsp;

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.280
&nbsp;
The pontianak are Southeast Asia’s infamous

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.280
vampiric mothers.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:19.060
A staple of Malay folklore thriving
even today, they share their name with a city

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.910
in Indonesia—in fact the city was actually
named after the monster.&nbsp;

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.050
&nbsp;
There are a lot of female monsters who use

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.540
femininity as a weapon.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:33.010
But this one is the first I’ve seen whose
monstrous nature is directly associated with

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.609
pregnancy and childbirth.

00:00:34.609 --> 00:00:41.179
But why does this gruesomely powerful, vampiric,
spectre of maternity gone awry haunt the jungle?

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:48.309
What is her story, and why is there a need
for this sort of monster?&nbsp;

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:51.449
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:56.120
The ghost of a woman who died tragically while
pregnant or during childbirth, the pontianak

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.550
seeks revenge against those who were involved
in her death.&nbsp;

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.250
Known alternatively as the kuntilanak, this
type of vengeful maternal spectre haunts&nbsp;

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:10.330
a vast region, including modern-day Malaysia,
Indonesia, Singapore, Brunei, all the way

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.830
down to the southern regions of the Philippines
and Thailand.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:18.090
Which means A LOT of people share the stories
of this monster!

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.401
&nbsp;
Both “pontianak” and “kuntilanak”

00:01:20.401 --> 00:01:24.040
end in the Malay word [“anak” ] meaning
“child” emphasizing they are literally

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.770
named and defined by their connection to children
and childbirth&nbsp;

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:33.510
Nourished by human blood and organs, especially
from children, the pontianak live in trees

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:38.000
and if you dare go out walking alone at night,
you might see them stalking prey from the

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.050
branches above.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.810
The favorite habitat of the pontianak is also
part of its name.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.930
It is said that the ‘Ponti’ in pontianak
originates from the Malay phrase for ‘tall

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.250
tree’ or ‘tall trees.’&nbsp;

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.670
&nbsp;
Although these monsters manifest as vampiric

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.380
ghosts, the pontianak can disguise herself
in the physical form of a beautiful young

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:03.330
woman who seems fragile and delicate, a façade
she uses to lure her victims in.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:08.409
In both human and ghost form, the long, flowing
black hair of the creature creates a stark

00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:10.329
contrast against its white robes.

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:14.610
When in their spectral form they are vicious
and gruesome.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.920
You can hear them “swooshing” or making
a rustling sound “like leaves crackling”

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.170
as they glide through the sky, followed by
a loud shrieking sound right before attacking

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.189
and drinking the blood of the victim.

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:30.920
They may use long fingernails or fangs to
create a wound from which they suck the blood

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.489
of their prey.

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:36.269
Some say these ghosts’ hair covers a hole
in the back of their necks.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:41.160
The last thing you will see as you succumb
to an attack is the scars of a snarling, scowling

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.719
face as she goes in for the kill.&nbsp;

00:02:43.719 --> 00:02:45.970
&nbsp;
The pontianak is a tortured female body in

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:51.390
part because it is trapped between the warring
influences of Islam and traditional Malay

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.459
cultural and religious practices.

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:58.150
Islam arrived in Southeast Asia as early as
the ninth century through Arabic traders,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:03.079
but the religion did not have a dramatic influence
in this part of the world until the 12th century

00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:04.079
CE.

00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:08.080
Muslim settlements arose as the trade routes
were solidified, and eventually led to the

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.670
conversion of various Malaysian monarchies
to Islam and the first sultans of the region.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:16.980
Of course, anytime you introduce a&nbsp; religion
and new rulers, you get tension with existing

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:22.860
beliefs, often manifesting in conflict between
perceived modernity and indigenous culture.&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.989
The pontianak is one such example.

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:31.140
In Islamic ideals, the female body is heteropatriarchal,
in deep contrast with the more egalitarian

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.019
Malay indigenous gender traditions.

00:03:33.019 --> 00:03:38.890
But the very thing that defines the identity
of a woman in both cultures, motherhood, is

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:42.230
what enables the supernatural existence of
these ghosts.&nbsp;

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:48.390
The pontianak is both seductive and repulsive,
a creature to desire and fear—she is monstrously

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.569
feminine.&nbsp;

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:54.459
Not all women who die during childbirth or
while pregnant become a pontianak—only those

00:03:54.459 --> 00:03:56.260
who suffer at the hands of men.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:01.719
That is one of the defining aspects of this
monster: The “unhappy death” in pontianak

00:04:01.719 --> 00:04:07.340
stories is a defining aspect of the monster,
and is brought on by sexual or, physical violence

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.169
during pregnancy, or outright murder.

00:04:10.169 --> 00:04:15.760
So this tormented ghost woman embodies two
of the most defining fears in much of women’s

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.530
history: patriarchal violence and the dangers
of childbirth.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:22.300
&nbsp;
In their ghostly form, pontianak are strong,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.700
scale tall trees, and fly.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:29.039
A nail or stake driven into the back of the
neck, subdues the monster, forcing it into

00:04:29.039 --> 00:04:34.009
its human form and making it the slave of
the assailant—often with a transformation

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:36.740
into a dutiful, quiet mother and wife.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.990
Removing the nail allows her to turn back
into her ghost form, but she cannot do this

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.169
herself.

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:45.060
&nbsp;
Not just a representation of women’s status

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.319
in the Malay and Islamic cultures out of which
it arose, pontianak play a part in the history

00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:52.860
of the very civilizations of the people who
share its story.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:58.960
The city of Pontianak in what is now Indonesia
founded in 1771 is said to have been the direct

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.080
result of a sultan expelling the monsters
that gave the place its name.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.889
According to the legend, the first inhabitants
of the land were the pontianak.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:11.370
A nobleman of Arab descent [Syarif Abdurrahman]
traveled to the area in the late 18th century

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:16.310
to settle the land after receiving it as a
gift, making him its first sultan.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:21.080
The native pontianak did not appreciate this
intrusion to homeland, and harassed the group

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.759
of settlers in an effort to scare them off.

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:29.020
The sultan retaliated by firing a cannon and
the loud noise drove away the ghosts.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:33.759
To make sure they would not return, the sultan
cut down the tall trees the pontianak called

00:05:33.759 --> 00:05:37.630
home, and used the wood to build a mosque
and his palace.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:42.199
To some people this story as an allegory of
the trials settlers faced attempting to tame

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:47.260
the dense jungle and its animal inhabitants,
but it seems a truer parallel comes from another

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.419
source of traditional folklore: pirates.

00:05:49.419 --> 00:05:55.389
What would become the city of Pontianak was
located at a delta of the Kapuas River, a&nbsp;

00:05:55.389 --> 00:05:57.229
crucial point of trade for the island of Borneo.

00:05:57.229 --> 00:06:02.220
The establishment of a settlement there to
deter pirates was the official narrative given

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:07.220
for the sultan’s appearance---the gang of
unruly ghosts was an added bonus deterrent.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:11.599
This founding myth of Pontianak emphasizes
the continuing tension between the spiritual

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:17.470
practices of the peoples of Southeast Asia,
like Buddhism, Hinduism, and animism, before

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.070
Islam became the dominant faith.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.890
Animism undoubtedly played a role in the formation
of the pontianak legend.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:28.699
Animism is essentially the belief that all
things, living and not living, have a spirit,

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:32.820
and that the material and spiritual worlds
are not distinct.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:38.080
That idea is so ingrained in Malay culture
that even now, regardless of religion, the

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:44.280
Pontianak is not just a monster story but
for some people it is an actual monster.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.250
The influence of interactions between humans
and non-human beings continues to hold deep

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:53.790
cultural sway—which helps explain why the
pontianak has existed for so long.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:55.509
&nbsp;
Not only does she have staying power as a

00:06:55.509 --> 00:07:00.860
cultural and historical figure, the symbolism
of the pontianak and the importance of storytelling

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:06.270
in Malaysian culture meant that the monster’s
transition from oral folklore to film was

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.270
inevitable.&nbsp;

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.400
&nbsp;
The horror genre has been an important facet

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.889
of the Malaysian film industry since the 1950s.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:17.321
Films that feature spirits in the horror genre
are called hantu films—and the pontianak

00:07:17.321 --> 00:07:19.720
made an early appearance in these movies.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.219
&nbsp;
However, when Singapore separated from Malaysia

00:07:22.219 --> 00:07:28.259
in 1965, urbanization and modern advancement
made the older traditions, like belief in

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:31.990
the pontianak, seem antiquated and out of
touch.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:37.590
Then government censorship policies in the
late 1960s banned the horror genre in its

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.479
entirety, and pontianak films became scarce.

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:45.569
They emerged again&nbsp; in the 1990s, but largely
as direct- to- video films.

00:07:45.569 --> 00:07:49.960
And then in 2004 came the release of the Pontianak
of the Tuber Rose [Pontianak Harum Sundal

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:50.960
Malam].

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.419
The film marked not just the return of the
pontianak to the big screen—but the return

00:07:54.419 --> 00:08:00.330
of the Malaysian blockbuster horror film itself,
after the relaxation of the “horror film”

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:01.330
restrictions.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.770
&nbsp;
Mirroring many of the previous legends of

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:07.999
the monster, the film alternates between 1949
and the present day, showing the pontianak

00:08:07.999 --> 00:08:10.490
in both colonial and modern periods.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:15.770
As in all pontianak lore, the titular character,
Meriam, is pregnant when her scorned lover

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.100
arranges for her to be attacked, robbed, and
murdered.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:26.749
Justice personified, the dead mother returns
to exact revenge on him and his family.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:31.560
While brutal, the film is also poignant; even
in the 21st century we see how women's power

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:37.099
is often confined and constrained to the very
specific and limited spheres of motherhood,

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:42.000
victimhood, sexuality, male relationships,
and vengeance.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.820
&nbsp;
As more and more horror films and novels featuring

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.959
the pontianak are made and distributed globally,
elements of Malaysian culture are becoming

00:08:49.959 --> 00:08:51.870
more broadly recognized.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:58.000
Malaysian culture was, and in many ways still
is, structured around oral tradition and narrative

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.000
storytelling.&nbsp;

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:04.610
Tales of the pontianak embody an entire, deeply
and uniquely female journey from loving, pregnant

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:11.339
mother to victim of horrific violence to vengeful
wraith back to enslaved and submissive wife.

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:14.610
Which is quite frankly, a terrible cycle.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:20.290
This monster truly embodies so many of the
divergent and conflicting elements of women’s

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:28.700
existence throughout history.

